[Music]  the witcher 3 has  dozens of haunting and disturbing  monsters but you might not know that the  inspiration for many of them  lie in some really strange gnarly  folklore  diving into mythology and seeing what  exactly medieval europeans were most  afraid of can really shed light on what  makes the fantastical creatures of the  witcher  so haunting haunting at least when  you're not totally distracted with side  quests  lost your key ma'am no not me k  me pan playing gwent and talking to pigs  all right let's talk  there may be minor spoilers for in-game  quests ahead but let's dive right in and  start the story  botchlings these grotesque zombies are  the tragic result of an improperly  buried child after it has died  hunting pregnant women at night the  botchling drains the mother of her  strength before brutally killing both  her  and the fetus you might think it'd be no  problem for the white wolf to defeat an  evil ghost baby but if a giant frog  proved to be a tough opponent  you may want to reconsider attacking the  botchling in the family matters main  quest  when threatened the footlong botchling  can change form growing into a deformed  powerful man botchlings are based  partially on  milings the ghosts of discarded or  unbaptized  infants in scandinavian folklore mylinks  were said to have roamed the land and  jumped on the backs of lone travelers  demanding to be taken to a graveyard and  buried properly  but as the traveler neared a graveyard  the myling became heavier and heavier  and more difficult to carry  and if the traveler couldn't make it  before the myling's patience was up it  would ragefully kill them  similar creatures that also inspired the  botchlings were the slavic pyronic and  drokovic  which were deceased children or infants  that similarly turned into hostile  demons when abandoned  however if it was given a proper burial  at the family home  instead of becoming wandering demons  they would become a protective house  spirit much like how if botchlings are  given a proper name through an elven  naming ritual their curse can be lifted  turning them into a docile spirit called  a lubricant  it's generally assumed these folktales  about vengeful myelins and peronic exist  to discourage people from committing the  sadly common middle-aged  practices of child abandonment and  infanticide  even hansel and gretel is about the  dangers that children face out on their  own  i definitely say that the in-game  origins of botchlings and the folklore  they're based in  are equally tragic and unsettling noon  wraiths  when the sun reached its highest point  in the sky on a hot summer day  farmers on the brink of exhaustion were  said to have visions of dancing maidens  that drew them in  and killed them that's dance encountered  in the contract quest  devil by the well and the white lady the  inspiration for noon wraiths directly  lie in slavic folklore  known in poland as poludnica meaning  lady midday  this mythological creature takes the  form of whirling clouds of dust and  carries a scythe and shears  on hot days in rural fields the spirit  attempts to start conversations with  delirious field workers and farmers  but if anyone fails to answer her or  tries to change the subject  she'll decapitate them with her scythe  or strike them with a heart attack  these haunting creatures are now widely  believed to basically be the  personification of heat stroke that  field laborers risk suffering  symptoms of heat stroke include  hallucinations and seeing or hearing  imaginary people  so that makes sense however this fear of  lady midday somewhat led to the practice  of taking breaks during the day to avoid  her wrath and  avoid exhaustion but geralt sure doesn't  seem to have a problem with facing noon  wraiths  visually similar to noon rates and  similar to fight plague maidens in the  witcher 3  are spirits that personify disease and  pestilence  annabelle encountered in the quest a  tower full of mice is a spirit revealed  to be a plague maiden after she was  eaten alive by  rats being rendered defenseless by a  paralyzing potion during a riot  that is a rough demise in the witcher  the catriona plague also known as the  red  death swept through the northern  kingdoms much like the bubonic plague  did in europe  the bestiary describes catriona diseased  patients is having  visions of a decrepit ghost resembling  an old woman  covered in scabs and boils as stated  eloquently in the witcher 3 art book  her rotten worm-ridden flesh is covered  in a ragged shroud seemingly woven from  miasma and foul  vapors crawling out from under it are  dozens of plague-bearing rats  yikes that's some very specific language  i feel like i can smell the plague  maiden from here  plague maidens are closely inspired by  the scandinavian spirit  pesta the physical embodiment of the  bubonic plague  the word pesta or pest is literally  norwegian for plague  the spirit of pesto was an old woman  dressed in black  that wiped out entire populations with  illness including a third of denmark's  population  and nearly half of norway's in just four  years during the 14th century  pesta traveled from village to town  bringing and spreading the black plague  with her  it was said that if you saw her carrying  a rake you'd know she'd only take a few  lives but if you saw her sweeping with  her broomstick  there would be mass fatalities of the  many creatures in the witcher universe  the noon wraiths and plague maidens are  some of the most loyal to their folklore  and some of the most creepy  if you've played the witcher you know  there's a bit of nudity so be warned for  the next section  alps who could forget the screeching  blood covered vampires from the blood  and wine expansion that creep around on  the tips of their toes  alps are based in germanic folklore and  are actually similar to elves  at least the more traditional morbid  idea of elves  alps in the witcher and alps in folklore  are both gray-skinned shape-shifters  with pointy ears but they're typically  male  while their female counterparts are  called mare or mara  similar to their in-game abilities  middle-aged folklore describes alps  seeking out sleeping targets in the  night and twisting their dreams into  horrific nightmares  in fact even the german word for  nightmare alpdrum  literally means elf dream alps perch on  the chest of their sleeping victims and  become heavier and heavier until the  victim wakes up being crushed  unable to move or breathe under the alps  weight  interestingly enough these attacks from  creatures across different cultures are  so prevalent  it's now believed that these were early  explanations for sleep paralysis  which is when a person wakes up feeling  totally unable to move or breathe  as well as possibly sleep apnea night  tears or lucid dreams  but alps also happen to like pulling  pranks like magically making milk turn  sour or  tangling people's hair into elf knots or  putting babies back into soiled  diapers wait what crushing farm animals  to death  and even drinking blood from the nipples  of men and young children  yeah okay maybe it's best that the alps  in the witcher don't stick completely to  their folklore but they are definitely  still creepy in their own  way the witcher 3 has a complex world  that aims for both fantasy  and realism a senior environment artist  on the game jonas matson even said  we don't rely on the classic fantasy  tropes when we create our world  we create it based off how people used  to live and what they used to believe  some of the truly horrifying qualities  of the game's monsters may be  as horrifying as they are because  they're based on real fears and real  human experiences  hopefully no one actually had a vampire  elf on their chest giving them  nightmares but  you never know either way the witcher 3  without a doubt has some seriously  creepy  quirky and unforgettable enemies  [Music]  you

